/P 534 0 R
/E (A compelling personality is not necessarily loud, dominant, mysterious, or endlessly entertaining. It is coherent. Your words, behavior, values, and pace generally point in the same direction. You do not have to perform confidence in one moment and then seek approval in the next.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
757 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (This is why imitation is usually a poor strategy. If you copy the mannerisms of a charismatic person, the surface may look convincing for a few minutes, but the underlying behavior eventually exposes the mismatch. A quieter person can be compelling through attentiveness. A naturally playful person can be  compelling through wit. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
758 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (Attraction is rarely experienced as a spreadsheet of desirable traits.  It is usually felt as a pattern: a person seems easy to be around, interesting to listen to, emotionally steady, or unusually alive. Personality matters because it changes the quality of the interaction itself. Two people can look equally polished and say equally intelligent things, yet one leaves the conversation feeling lighter while the other leaves it feeling managed.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
759 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 534 0 R
/E (That difference often comes from small signals rather than a single magnetic quality. Warmth tells someone she can relax. Curiosity tells her that her inner world matters. A clear point of view signals that you have a self rather than simply adapting to whoever is in front of you. Humor can lower tension. Emotional steadiness makes disagreement safer. None of these guarantees attraction, but together they create conditions in which attraction can develop naturally.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
