/P 536 0 R
/E (Healthy curiosity is an invitation. Manipulative curiosity is bait. An invitation gives information that can be explored voluntarily. Bait withholds information specifically to produce anxiety, jealousy, or pursuit. Posting a deliberately cryptic photograph so someone wonders whether you are with another woman is not sophisticated mystery; it is manufactured uncertainty.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
776 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 536 0 R
/E (The difference matters because the emotional mechanism is different. One creates interest through discovery. The other creates attention through discomfort. If your method only works when the other person misunderstands your intention, the method deserves scrutiny.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
777 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 536 0 R
/E (Curiosity grows when there is something meaningful left to discover. That does not require deliberate secrecy. It can be as simple as not explaining every thought before the other person has had a chance to ask.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
778 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 536 0 R
/E (Compare two answers to the question, 'What did you do this weekend?' One is a complete itinerary. The other is, 'I tried something I had never done before. It went much better than expected.' The second answer is not manipulative. It simply leaves a natural opening for a follow-up question. If she asks, you tell the story. If she changes the subject, you let it go.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
