/P 579 0 R
/E (Robert Greene's The Art of Seduction \(2001\) is one of the most in fluential books ever written on this subject, and it deserves a real, honest treatment rather than a dismissal  partly because you will encounter its ideas again, recycled and relabeled, all over dating content online whether or not the source is ever named. Greene built the book by studying history's most famous seducers ? Cleopatra, Casanova, Josephine Bonaparte, John F. Kennedy gaining what he found into a set of archetypes: the Siren, the Rake, the Ideal Lover, the Dandy, the Natural, the Coquette, the Charmer, the Charismatic. His central claim is that seduction is a form of power, that nearly everyone secretly wants the ability to wield it, and that it can be studied and practiced like any other skill. Worth noting up front, because it tells you something about the book's whole orientation: Greene's own working term for the person being seduced is "the victim.")
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
