/E (Don't confuse a small pool with scarcity. It can feel, in a specific dorm or major or friend group, like there are only a few people worth dating and everyone's already tried. Zoom out. Your actual pool is much larger than your visible one.)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 590 0 R
/E (College is, in some ways, the easiest environment you'll ever date in  and in other ways, one of the trickiest. Easiest, because you're surrounded by people your age with unusually high amounts of unstructured social time. Trickiest, because reputations travel fast in a small community, and because the pool feels both huge and small at once. Use your environment instead of fighting it. Clubs, classes, study groups, and shared living situations are all natural, repeated-exposure settings  research on attraction consistently shows that familiarity and repeated low-stakes contact build comfort and liking more reliably than a single high-pressure interaction. You don't need to manufacture a big moment; showing up consistently in the same spaces does more work. Reputation is currency protect it deliberately. How you talk about women when they're not in the room, how you treat people after things don't work out, and how you handle rejections?)
/Pg 83 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
